scripts/social.sh now reads its per-run spend from the usage record in the audit log instead of the ↳ line. In production that line never reaches the log, so every social run, including ones that finished, reported no_terminal_emit.

What was wrong: social.sh captures yoyo's output with 2>&1 | tee. In that setup yoyo switches itself to quiet mode (src/cli.rs), and quiet mode drops the ↳ usage line. The old grep could never match, so every social run reported it had died before finishing. Run 36463335955 did exactly that on a run that completed.

Changes:
• **`scripts/social.sh** (commit 20cbaff0): the reader looks for "type":"usage"` records among the audit lines written during this run. It prints how many there are and each one, because social.sh can call the binary more than once. It prints no_terminal_emit only when this run wrote no usage record at all. The Spend audit: line is unchanged, byte for byte, and the function still never changes the script's exit status. I also fixed the header comment, which said the ↳ line gets captured into the log.
• **`tests/harness_logic.sh** (commit e5cf1d30): four new cases, all with no ↳` line in the log, as in production:
  • **A:** a finished run reports its usage record and does not say no_terminal_emit.
  • **B:** a run that calls yoyo twice reports both records and a count of 2.
  • C: a run that died before finishing reports no_terminal_emit.
  • D: a usage record left by an earlier run is not counted as this run's.

Checks:
• Positive control: in one command I put the old grep back, ran the harness and restored the file. The old code failed 6 checks, including Case A by name (136 passed, 6 failed). After restoring: 142 passed, 0 failed, and no neutered markers are left.
• `cargo build, cargo test, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings and cargo fmt --check` all pass. No Rust code changed.

I commented on #944 with the production run and the fix. The other half of that issue is still open: nothing saves the spend anywhere a later session reads. It is only printed in the run log.
